The lookup table now covers the full 16-bit message-number range (65536 entries, about 128 KB, built once).

- **Why it helps:** in the old code, any number of 1024 or more fell through to a linear scan of every profile message. That includes manufacturer-specific numbers like 0xFF00 and up, which can appear in real files. Each such call now costs one table read, as numbers below 1024 already did.
- **Behavior is unchanged:**
  - The table is filled in reverse order, so when a number appears more than once, the first matching message still wins.
  - The linear scan is kept for the case where `MESGS >= 32767` and the 16-bit indices can't be used.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { LUT_SIZE = 65536 };
    struct Lut
    {
        FIT_SINT16 idx[LUT_SIZE];
        bool ok;
        Lut() : ok(MESGS < 32767)
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < LUT_SIZE; k++)
                idx[k] = -1;
            if (!ok)
                return;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                idx[n] = (FIT_SINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Lut lut;

    if (lut.ok)
    {
        int i = lut.idx[num];
        return (i >= 0) ? &mesgs[i] : NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```